import java.util.Scanner;

/*
 ============================================================================
  LAB PRACTICE III (LP-3) - DESIGN AND ANALYSIS OF ALGORITHMS (DAA)
  ASSIGNMENT 5: N-QUEENS PROBLEM (BACKTRACKING STRATEGY)
 ============================================================================

 PROBLEM STATEMENT:
 The N-Queens puzzle is the problem of placing N chess queens on an N x N 
 chessboard such that no two queens threaten each other.
 Thus, a solution requires that no two queens share the same row, column, 
 or diagonal.

 ALGORITHMIC STRATEGY: BACKTRACKING (DEPTH FIRST SEARCH WITH PRUNING)
 - Place queens one by one in different rows, starting from row 0.
 - Before placing a queen at board[row][col], check if the position is safe:
     * Check if another queen exists in the same column (above).
     * Check upper-left diagonal.
     * Check upper-right diagonal.
   (No need to check lower rows/diagonals since queens are placed sequentially from row 0 to N-1).
 - If safe:
     * Mark board[row][col] = 1 (Place queen).
     * Recursively try placing queens in the next row (row + 1).
     * If placement in subsequent rows leads to no valid solution, BACKTRACK:
       reset board[row][col] = 0 and try the next column.
 - Base Condition: When row == N, all N queens have been placed successfully.
 ============================================================================
*/

public class NQueens {

    static int n; // Board dimension (n x n) and number of queens

    /**
     * Bounding / Safety Function:
     * Validates whether placing a queen at board[row][col] violates chess rules.
     * 
     * @param board current state of chessboard (1 represents queen, 0 is empty)
     * @param row target row index
     * @param col target column index
     * @return true if position is safe from attack; false otherwise
     */
    static boolean isSafe(int[][] board, int row, int col) {

        // 1. Check vertical column above the current row
        for (int i = 0; i < row; i++) {
            if (board[i][col] == 1)
                return false;
        }

        // 2. Check upper-left diagonal (\ direction)
        for (int i = row - 1, j = col - 1; i >= 0 && j >= 0; i--, j--) {
            if (board[i][j] == 1)
                return false;
        }

        // 3. Check upper-right diagonal (/ direction)
        for (int i = row - 1, j = col + 1; i >= 0 && j < n; i--, j++) {
            if (board[i][j] == 1)
                return false;
        }

        // Position is safe from all existing queens
        return true;
    }

    /**
     * Recursive Backtracking Function:
     * Attempts to place a queen row by row.
     * 
     * @param board n x n chessboard matrix
     * @param row current row index to place a queen
     * @return true if all queens placed successfully; false if dead-end reached
     */
    static boolean solve(int[][] board, int row) {

        // BASE CASE: All N queens are successfully placed on rows 0 to n-1
        if (row == n)
            return true;

        // Try placing queen in all columns of the current row one by one
        for (int col = 0; col < n; col++) {

            // Check if column 'col' in 'row' is safe
            if (isSafe(board, row, col)) {

                // DO: Place queen at (row, col)
                board[row][col] = 1;

                // RECURSE: Move to next row
                if (solve(board, row + 1))
                    return true;

                // UNDO (BACKTRACK): If placing queen here doesn't lead to solution, remove it
                board[row][col] = 0;
            }
        }

        // If queen cannot be placed in any column of this row, trigger backtracking
        return false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter number of Queens (N): ");
        n = sc.nextInt();

        // Initialize empty N x N chessboard (all zeros)
        int[][] board = new int[n][n];

        // Start backtracking from row 0
        if (solve(board, 0)) {

            System.out.println("\nOne Valid Chessboard Solution (1 = Queen, 0 = Empty):");
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                    System.out.print((board[i][j] == 1 ? " Q " : " . "));
                }
                System.out.println();
            }

        } else {
            System.out.println("No solution exists for N = " + n);
        }

        sc.close();
    }
}

/*
 ============================================================================
                         THEORY & ARCHITECTURE
 ============================================================================

 1. WHAT IS BACKTRACKING?
    Backtracking is an algorithmic technique for solving problems recursively 
    by trying to build a solution incrementally, one piece at a time.
    Whenever it determines that the current partial candidate cannot possibly 
    lead to a valid complete solution, it abandons the candidate ("backtracks") 
    by undoing the previous move and exploring the next alternative.

 2. BRUTE FORCE VS BACKTRACKING:
    - Brute Force: Generates all possible configurations of placing N queens 
      on an N x N board (N^N states or C(N^2, N) states) and checks each one.
      For N = 8: 8^8 = 16,777,216 combinations!
    - Backtracking: Prunes invalid subtrees immediately. If queen in row 1 
      attacks queen in row 0, the entire branch is discarded without evaluating 
      rows 2 to 7. Only 2,057 nodes are explored for N = 8!

 3. COMPLEXITY ANALYSIS:
    - Time Complexity: O(N!) Upper Bound.
      In row 0, there are N choices. In row 1, at most N - 2 safe choices. 
      In row 2, at most N - 4 safe choices. 
      Total work: N * (N - 2) * (N - 4) ... = O(N!).
    - Auxiliary Space Complexity:
      * 2D Matrix: O(N^2)
      * Call Stack Depth: O(N) recursion stack frames.
      * Overall auxiliary space: O(N^2).

 4. HOW TO OPTIMIZE `isSafe` FROM O(N) TO O(1)?
    Instead of looping across columns and diagonals, use 3 boolean lookup arrays:
    1. boolean[] cols = new boolean[N];
       -> checks column conflict.
    2. boolean[] mainDiag = new boolean[2 * N - 1];
       -> checks '\' diagonal using index: (row - col + N - 1).
    3. boolean[] antiDiag = new boolean[2 * N - 1];
       -> checks '/' diagonal using index: (row + col).
    This reduces the safety check from O(N) to O(1) time per cell.

