 Q1: What is the N-Queens problem?
 A1: It is the challenge of placing N non-attacking queens on an N x N chessboard 
     such that no two queens share the same row, column, or diagonal.

 Q2: Which algorithmic design paradigm is used to solve N-Queens?
 A2: Backtracking (Systematic exhaustive search via Depth First Search with pruning).

 Q3: Why don't we need to check rows in `isSafe`?
 A3: Because our recursive logic places exactly ONE queen per row sequentially 
     (row + 1 at each recursion step). Hence, two queens can never be in the same row.

 Q4: Why don't we check lower diagonals or rows below `row` in `isSafe`?
 A4: Because queens are placed from top to bottom (row 0 to N-1). Rows below the 
     current row are empty and contain no queens yet.

 Q5: What is the time complexity of the N-Queens backtracking algorithm?
 A5: O(N!) in the worst case, because the number of choices decreases by at least 
     1 or 2 at each row level.

 Q6: What is the space complexity of this program?
 A6: O(N^2) for the chessboard matrix and O(N) for the recursive call stack depth.

 Q7: For what values of N does NO solution exist?
 A7: For N = 2 and N = 3, no solution exists. Solutions exist for N = 1 and all N >= 4.

 Q8: How many total solutions exist for standard 8-Queens?
 A8: There are 92 total solutions (representing 12 unique/fundamental solutions 
     when rotational and reflectional symmetries are factored out).

 Q9: What is a State-Space Tree?
 A9: A tree representing all possible states explored by a backtracking algorithm. 
     The root represents the empty board, level 1 represents positions of Queen 0, 
     level 2 represents Queen 1, and so on.

 Q10: What is a Bounding Function / Pruning?
 A10: A bounding function checks whether a path in the state-space tree satisfies 
      problem constraints (here `isSafe`). If constraints are violated, pruning kills 
      the subtree immediately without further exploration.

 Q11: How can the safety check be made O(1) constant time?
 A11: By maintaining three boolean tracking arrays or bitmasks:
      - `cols[col]`
      - `slashDiagonal[row + col]`
      - `backslashDiagonal[row - col + N - 1]`.

 Q12: How can we modify the code to count and print ALL solutions instead of just one?
 A12: In the base condition `if (row == n)`, print the board and increment a global 
      counter, then return `false` (or don't return `true`) so the loop continues 
      exploring other columns and branches.

 Q13: Can N-Queens be solved in 1D array representation?
 A13: Yes, an array `int[] board = new int[N]` where `board[i] = col` indicates a 
      queen at row i and column col. Two queens at (r1, c1) and (r2, c2) conflict if:
      c1 == c2 OR |r1 - r2| == |c1 - c2|.

 Q14: What is the difference between Backtracking and Branch & Bound?
 A14: - Backtracking uses Depth First Search (DFS) with a bounding function; 
       well-suited for decision and constraint satisfaction problems.
     - Branch and Bound uses Breadth First Search (BFS) or Best-First Search with 
       a cost function; well-suited for optimization problems (e.g., Min/Max Cost).

 Q15: What are practical real-world applications of N-Queens and Backtracking?
 A15: - Constraint Satisfaction Problems (CSP)
